Starting this project proved to be more challenging than I had anticipated. At first, it was difficult to decide where to concentrate because Ontario farm financing impacts economics, equality, rural policy, and statistics all at once. I didn't realize how much that early framing would influence everything that came after, and it required additional iterations to get the framing perfect in Milestone 1. The biggest surprise to me was how much the systems thinking component altered my interpretation of the facts. It's one thing to look at agricultural consolidation as a spreadsheet number. The problem seemed really different when it was mapped as a feedback loop, showing why the system continues to produce that result on its own, without anyone's intention. If I were to start over, I would devote more time to the data wrangling stage in order to provide a clearer basis for the subsequent analysis. Data cleansing with Statistics Canada datasets, systems mapping, and converting technical results into understandable suggestions for a non-technical audience are among the skills I acquired. I found this to be quite difficult but useful.